  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "An electric motor turns electrical energy into rotation to power fans, tools and machines. Inside the motor, the essential interaction is between electric current and a magnetic field. Two fixed magnets create a magnetic field across the motor's central space. A wire coil sits in that field, and the motor sends electric current through the coil. Current in the coil produces opposite forces on its two sides, creating a turning force called torque. torque. Those forces rotate the coil and the shaft attached to it. But the coil would stall if the current stayed in the same direction. A split ring commutator reverses the current every half turn, keeping the torque in the same rotational direction. So the motor keeps spinning by repeatedly switching the coil's current so magnetic forces continue turning its shaft.",
  "mean_word_probability": 0.9757534989077061,
